// Original work, written for tests/fixtures/nonlinear-timeline (temporal-
// consistency mechanism material, 2026-08-03). Flashback-framed structure:
// a deathbed present tense frame with two FLASHBACK insertions (one a
// longer multi-scene continuous run, one a short single scene), each
// returning via a CONTINUOUS-marked scene, per the markers
// extractTemporalConstraints() actually parses (heading-only FLASHBACK /
// CONTINUOUS). Temporally clean as written -- 0 expected contradictions.

INT. RIVERBEND HOSPICE - ROOM FOUR - DAY

Late afternoon light through half-closed blinds. IRENA KOVAC, elderly, propped on pillows, an oxygen line looped under her nose. Her granddaughter, NOOR, twenties, sits close, holding a chipped enamel pin between two fingers.

NOOR
You never told me what this was for. Just that I wasn't allowed to touch it.

IRENA
(a thin smile)
Now you're allowed. Everything I was keeping it from you for already happened.

INT. RIVERBEND HOSPICE - ROOM FOUR - DAY

Noor turns the pin over in her palm -- a small tarnished star.

NOOR
Tell me, then. All of it, not the version from the birthday toasts.

Irena closes her eyes, not from pain -- from arriving somewhere.

IRENA
The version from the toasts starts with a road. It should start with a fence.

EXT. VILLAGE FENCE LINE - DAWN - FLASHBACK

Frost on wire. YOUNG IRENA, seventeen, presses a folded paper into a gap in the fence post while a dog barks somewhere behind her, close.

YOUNG IRENA
(barely a whisper)
Don't you dare bark now. Not now.

She freezes, listens, then moves fast into the tree line.

EXT. FOREST EDGE - CONTINUOUS

Young Irena runs low along a ditch, breath fogging, one hand pressed to her side.

A distant shout, unintelligible, behind her. She doesn't look back.

INT. FARMHOUSE CELLAR - CONTINUOUS

A trapdoor opens from below. Young Irena drops down into darkness, pulling it shut over her head. A man's hand, unseen owner, presses a lantern into hers.

MAN'S VOICE (O.S.)
Two hours. Not a sound before that.

EXT. RIVER CROSSING - NIGHT - CONTINUOUS

Later that same unbroken night: Young Irena wades a shallow crossing, lantern extinguished, a bundle held over her head. On the far bank, a shape waits -- a boy, thin, no older than her.

BOY
(reaching to help her up)
You're the one with the star pin.

YOUNG IRENA
I'm the one who almost wasn't.

INT. RIVERBEND HOSPICE - ROOM FOUR - DAY - CONTINUOUS

Irena's eyes open. Noor hasn't moved, the pin still cupped in her hand like it might vanish.

IRENA
That boy carried messages for three years and never once told anyone my name.

NOOR
What happened to him?

IRENA
That's a different story. Ask me tomorrow, if there's a tomorrow to ask in.

INT. RIVERBEND HOSPICE - ROOM FOUR - DAY

A nurse passes the doorway, glances in, moves on without interrupting. Noor waits until the hallway is quiet again.

NOOR
Was there a day you almost got caught? A real one, not the fence.

IRENA
(a long breath)
Once. At the checkpoint. I'll tell you, but you have to let me tell it slow.

EXT. BORDER CHECKPOINT - NIGHT - FLASHBACK

A striped barrier arm, a single guard lamp swinging in wind. YOUNG IRENA, papers in hand, stands rigid as a GUARD examines them under the light, far too long.

GUARD
(not looking up)
This photograph is five years old.

YOUNG IRENA
(steady, rehearsed calm)
I was told not to smile in the new one. I look like a different person when I smile.

The guard finally looks up, studies her face against the photo.

INT. RIVERBEND HOSPICE - ROOM FOUR - DAY - CONTINUOUS

Irena's hand trembles slightly, remembering, and Noor covers it with her own without being asked.

IRENA
He waved me through. To this day I don't know if he believed me or simply wanted his shift to end.

NOOR
Grandma. You were seventeen.

INT. RIVERBEND HOSPICE - ROOM FOUR - DAY

Irena presses the star pin into Noor's palm, closes her granddaughter's fingers around it gently.

IRENA
Seventeen and certain I'd live forever. Turns out I only needed to live long enough to hand this to you.

Noor holds the pin against her chest, and for a long moment neither of them speaks.
